verdict.summary:
VERDICT

No. Under Resource Timing Level 2, an HTML IMG element whose src is a data: URI does NOT produce a PerformanceResourceTiming object in the Performance Timeline. It is not returned by performance.getEntriesByType("resource") / getEntries() / getEntriesByName(), and it is not delivered to a PerformanceObserver subscribed to entryType "resource".

1. DIRECT SPEC CITATION

The specification states this explicitly, in the Examples subsection of the PerformanceResourceTiming introduction:

  "If an HTML IMG element has a data: URI as its source [RFC2397], then this resource will not be included as a PerformanceResourceTiming object in the Performance Timeline. By definition data: URI contains embedded data and does not require a fetch."

  — Resource Timing (W3C), https://www.w3.org/TR/resource-timing/
  — Resource Timing Level 2, W3C Candidate Recommendation Draft 2022-11-10, https://www.w3.org/TR/2022/CRD-resource-timing-20221110/
  — Resource Timing Level 2, W3C Working Draft 2019-06-26, https://www.w3.org/TR/2019/WD-resource-timing-2-20190626/
  — Resource Timing Level 2, W3C Working Draft 2018-04-22, https://www.w3.org/TR/2018/WD-resource-timing-2-20180422/
  — Resource Timing Level 2, W3C Working Draft 2017-03-29 (and 2017-03-28), https://www.w3.org/TR/2017/WD-resource-timing-2-20170329/

The identical sentence, with identical wording, appears in every revision above. The answer therefore does not depend on which draft of Resource Timing Level 2 is consulted: the statement is stable from the first Level 2 Working Draft through the current /TR publication. Revision ambiguity is not a blocker here.

2. NORMATIVE BASIS (why the example is the correct reading, not just an editorial aside)

The data: URI sentence sits inside a block the spec introduces as "The rest of this section is non-normative." That matters for how strongly it binds a UA, so the answer should not rest on the example alone. It does not have to, because the same conclusion follows from the normative architecture:

  (a) Scope of the timeline. The introduction defines what the timeline contains as resources that are fetched and not excluded during the fetch: resources "are included as PerformanceResourceTiming objects in the client's global object's Performance Timeline, unless excluded from the timeline as part of the fetching process." Entry into the timeline is thus contingent on a fetch occurring.

  (b) The entry is created by the fetch machinery. Resource Timing does not independently observe HTML elements. It defines a processing model whose "add a resource timing entry"/queue steps are invoked from the Fetch standard's reporting hooks, i.e. from the HTTP fetch path. There is no parallel hook that watches the DOM for URL-bearing attributes.

  (c) data: URLs never enter that path. A data: URL is resolved by Fetch's scheme-fetch handling, which parses the URL, produces a response from the embedded bytes, and returns without performing HTTP fetch / HTTP-network-or-cache fetch. No network or cache fetch means no invocation of the reporting hook, which means no PerformanceResourceTiming object. This is exactly what the spec means by "does not require a fetch."

  (d) Consequence for attribute values, which corroborates (c). PerformanceResourceTiming is built around fetch milestones — fetchStart, domainLookupStart/End, connectStart/End, requestStart, responseStart, secureConnectionStart, transferSize, encodedBodySize, decodedBodySize, nextHopProtocol. A data: URI has none of these. Were an entry created it would be a degenerate record of zeros with an empty/opaque nextHopProtocol, carrying no diagnostic information. The spec resolves this by not creating the entry at all.

3. WHAT THE RULE DOES AND DOES NOT COVER

Not covered (i.e. these also yield no "resource" entry, for the same or related reasons):
  - CSS url(data:...) backgrounds, <script src="data:...">, <link href="data:...">, <video>/<audio> with data: sources, fetch("data:..."), XMLHttpRequest to a data: URL, <iframe src="data:..."> — the exclusion is a property of the scheme, not of IMG.
  - Resources blocked by a fetch precondition (mixed content, CORS, CSP) — a separate, explicitly stated exclusion in the same examples list.
  - Resources whose Timing-Allow-Origin check fails: they ARE in the timeline, but their detailed timings are zeroed. Do not conflate this with the data: case — TAO redaction produces an entry; data: produces none.

Still covered (so the verdict is not a general 'no timing for inline content' claim):
  - A blob: URL created from an in-memory Blob is still a fetchable URL and is not the data: case; treat it as out of scope of this verdict and verify empirically if it matters.
  - An IMG whose src is changed from a data: URI to an http(s) URL produces exactly one entry — for the http(s) fetch. Per the same examples list, "If the src attribute of a HTML IMG element is changed via script, both the fetch of the original resource as well as the fetch of the new URL would be included" — the data: leg contributes nothing because there was no fetch.
  - An http(s) image whose *response body* happens to be served from cache or from a service worker still gets an entry; caching is explicitly included.

Adjacent APIs that are NOT evidence about this question:
  - Largest Contentful Paint and Element Timing are separate entry types under separate specifications. An LCP candidate that is a data: URI image can appear as a largest-contentful-paint entry (with an empty or elided url, per that spec's own rules) while still producing zero "resource" entries. Do not read an LCP entry as contradicting this verdict.
  - DevTools' Network panel likewise does not list data: URLs as requests, which is consistent but is not the specification.

4. HOW TO VERIFY THIS IN A BROWSER (separate from the spec answer, but cheap)

Drop the following into a page served over http(s) (file:// works too, but serve over http so the control image is a real network fetch). It uses a 1x1 GIF data: URI as the probe and a same-directory PNG as a positive control, so a zero result is distinguishable from a broken harness.

<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>data: URI resource timing probe</title>
<img id="probe" alt=""
     src="data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhAQABAIAAAAAAAP///yH5BAEAAAAALAAAAAABAAEAAAIBRAA7">
<img id="control" alt="" src="./probe-control.png">
<script>
  // Observe live entries as well as anything already buffered.
  const seen = [];
  const po = new PerformanceObserver((list) => {
    for (const e of list.getEntries()) seen.push(e.name);
  });
  po.observe({ type: 'resource', buffered: true });

  addEventListener('load', () => {
    // Give the observer a task turn to flush.
    setTimeout(() => {
      const all = performance.getEntriesByType('resource').map(e => e.name);
      const dataEntries = all.filter(n => n.startsWith('data:'));
      const controlEntries = all.filter(n => n.includes('probe-control.png'));
      console.table({
        'resource entries (total)': all.length,
        'data: entries (expect 0)': dataEntries.length,
        'control entries (expect >=1)': controlEntries.length,
        'data: seen via observer (expect 0)': seen.filter(n => n.startsWith('data:')).length
      });
      console.log('data: names:', dataEntries);
    }, 0);
  });
</script>

Pass criteria: "data: entries" = 0 and "data: seen via observer" = 0, while "control entries" >= 1. The control is what makes the test falsifiable — if the control is also 0, the harness is wrong (image failed to load, entry type unsupported, buffer already cleared), not the specification. A single non-zero data: count with a passing control would be a UA deviation from the quoted spec text and should be recorded with browser name and version.

Equivalent one-liner for a quick check in any page that already contains a data: URI image:

  performance.getEntriesByType('resource').filter(e => e.name.startsWith('data:'))

Expected: an empty array.

5. BOUNDS ON THIS ANSWER

  - This is the specification answer. The data: sentence is in a non-normative examples block, so a conforming UA is not formally forbidden from emitting an entry; it is, however, the behavior the spec states and the behavior the fetch architecture produces. Browser-specific empirics are a distinct exercise; run section 4 per engine and per version if you need a compatibility matrix. Historically the engines agree with the spec here (no data: entries), but that should be confirmed, not assumed, for any version you intend to ship against.
  - The verdict assumes the IMG is in the main document's global object and that the data: URI is a valid, successfully decoded image. Error paths and worker/iframe scoping are out of scope and do not change the outcome for this scheme: a failed data: image is still not a fetch, so it is still absent from the timeline.
  - If you need a timing signal for an inline image, Resource Timing is the wrong tool. Use the element's load event timestamp, img.decode() resolution, or the Element Timing API (elementtiming attribute), each of which reports on the element rather than on a fetch.

